WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:20.153
Jim and I figured out that in three more days we would come to the town of Cairo,

2
00:00:21.193 --> 00:00:23.554
where the Ohio River joins the Mississippi.

3
00:00:24.415 --> 00:00:29.237
There we could sell our raft and buy tickets for a steamboat going up the Ohio River.

4
00:00:30.418 --> 00:00:35.224
It would take us to the North, where there was no slavery, and where Jim would be safe.

5
00:00:39.426 --> 00:00:41.346
The next night, a fog rolled in.

6
00:00:42.326 --> 00:00:45.207
We looked for a place on the riverbank to tie up our raft.

7
00:00:46.247 --> 00:00:48.668
I went ahead in the canoe with the raft's towline.

8
00:00:49.668 --> 00:00:56.090
I looped the line around a small tree and tied it tight, but the raft tore the tree

9
00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:57.911
out by the roots and kept on going.

10
00:01:00.711 --> 00:01:05.265
Now I was alone in the canoe, surrounded by a thick white blanket of fog.

11
00:01:06.345 --> 00:01:08.446
After a time, I heard a voice yelling.

12
00:01:09.646 --> 00:01:15.107
I paddled as fast as I could toward the sound, but when I heard the voice again, it

13
00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:16.988
seemed to be coming from another direction.

14
00:01:18.028 --> 00:01:18.968
I paddled some more.

15
00:01:20.149 --> 00:01:23.290
At last I was so tired that I fell asleep in the canoe.

16
00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:28.791
When I woke up, the fog had lifted.

17
00:01:30.471 --> 00:01:34.041
Ahead of me, I saw the raft, and there was Jim, asleep.

18
00:01:36.063 --> 00:01:38.585
I got on the raft and stretched out beside him.

19
00:01:39.506 --> 00:01:42.528
I said, "Hey, Jim, have you been asleep?"

20
00:01:43.849 --> 00:01:45.050
"Is that really you, Huck?"

21
00:01:45.791 --> 00:01:46.451
Jim exclaimed.

22
00:01:46.471 --> 00:01:49.333
"I thought you had drowned for sure!"

23
00:01:50.835 --> 00:01:52.756
I decided to play a joke on Jim.

24
00:01:53.857 --> 00:01:58.621
I told him that I'd been on the raft all along and that he had dreamed everything that

25
00:01:58.661 --> 00:01:59.562
happened in the fog.

26
00:02:00.582 --> 00:02:04.241
He said that if it was a dream, everything in it must mean something.

27
00:02:05.162 --> 00:02:11.086
The riverbank with the trees, he explained, was a man trying to help us, but the river current

28
00:02:11.146 --> 00:02:14.288
that pulled the raft away was a man trying to harm us.

29
00:02:15.889 --> 00:02:19.432
The fog meant all kinds of troubles that we'd have to steer away from.

30
00:02:19.452 --> 00:02:21.933
"And what do these things stand for?"

31
00:02:22.394 --> 00:02:27.037
I said, pointing to the leaves and trash that had collected on the raft in the fog.

32
00:02:28.078 --> 00:02:33.924
Jim looked at them for a long time, and then he said slowly, "I fell asleep because I

33
00:02:33.964 --> 00:02:35.765
was so tired from calling for you.

34
00:02:37.085 --> 00:02:40.746
I thought you were dead, and my heart was almost broken.

35
00:02:41.526 --> 00:02:45.107
When I woke up and saw you alive, I was full of joy.

36
00:02:46.128 --> 00:02:50.109
But you were only thinking about how you could make a fool of me with a lie.

37
00:02:51.149 --> 00:02:54.830
So what that trash stands for is people who make fun of their friends."

38
00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:58.511
I felt ashamed, and I told Jim I was sorry.

39
00:02:59.551 --> 00:03:01.652
I never played any mean tricks on him again.

40
00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:07.897
The next night, as we floated down the river, we talked again about the town of Cairo.

41
00:03:08.978 --> 00:03:11.038
I was worried we might pass it in the dark.

42
00:03:12.018 --> 00:03:17.460
Jim said that if we found Cairo, he'd soon be a free man, but if we missed it,

43
00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.661
we'd still be in the South, where black people are slaves.

44
00:03:21.801 --> 00:03:24.542
He talked about the things he would do when he reached the North.

45
00:03:25.482 --> 00:03:27.643
He'd get a job and save all his money.

46
00:03:28.603 --> 00:03:32.704
Then he would buy his wife from the man who owned her, so they could live together again.

47
00:03:34.332 --> 00:03:37.554
When we saw lights ahead, we tied up the raft on the riverbank.

48
00:03:38.534 --> 00:03:41.196
Then I took the canoe and paddled toward the town.

49
00:03:43.517 --> 00:03:47.759
I came to a man in a rowboat, and I asked him if the town ahead was Cairo.

50
00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:49.660
He told me it wasn't.

51
00:03:51.321 --> 00:03:54.963
Jim was very disappointed, but we set out on the raft again.

52
00:03:55.864 --> 00:03:59.265
Later that night we passed another town that was on high ground.

53
00:04:00.346 --> 00:04:03.908
We knew there was no high ground around Cairo, so we kept going.

54
00:04:04.992 --> 00:04:10.896
When daylight came, we could see the clear water of the Ohio River near the shoreline, so we

55
00:04:10.956 --> 00:04:15.459
knew we had passed the place where the clear Ohio and the muddy Mississippi join together.

56
00:04:15.479 --> 00:04:19.762
"Maybe we passed by Cairo that night in the fog," I said.

57
00:04:19.782 --> 00:04:23.085
"Let's not talk about it, Huck," he said.

58
00:04:23.105 --> 00:04:25.486
"It's just bad luck.

59
00:04:26.287 --> 00:04:29.409
I always thought that snakeskin wasn't done with us yet."

60
00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.431
"I'm so sorry I touched it, Jim!"

61
00:04:32.832 --> 00:04:33.152
I said.

62
00:04:34.028 --> 00:04:35.649
He told me not to blame myself.

63
00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:39.532
Now we had to turn around and go back upriver.

64
00:04:40.673 --> 00:04:44.856
The floating raft couldn't go upriver, so we would have to go in the canoe.

65
00:04:45.877 --> 00:04:50.840
We slept all day on the shore under the trees, but when we went back to the raft

66
00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:52.722
in the evening, the canoe was gone!

67
00:04:54.183 --> 00:04:56.425
Neither of us said a word for a long while.

68
00:04:57.385 --> 00:05:00.848
We both thought it was the snakeskin bringing us more bad luck.

69
00:05:01.924 --> 00:05:06.126
Finally, we decided that we would have to keep going downriver on the raft.

70
00:05:07.007 --> 00:05:10.129
Maybe we'd come to a town where we could buy another canoe.

71
00:05:11.029 --> 00:05:15.512
We set off into the night, but the snakeskin wasn't finished with its work yet.

72
00:05:16.612 --> 00:05:18.393
The fog closed in around us.

73
00:05:19.254 --> 00:05:25.097
We could hear a big steamboat coming in the other direction, with its huge stern wheel churning and

74
00:05:25.137 --> 00:05:29.980
pounding. We lit our lantern, but the steamboat was headed right for us.

75
00:05:31.008 --> 00:05:34.930
Jim jumped overboard off one side of the raft, and I jumped off the other.

76
00:05:36.051 --> 00:05:39.013
The steamboat tore into our raft and kept going.

77
00:05:40.434 --> 00:05:44.776
When I came to the surface, I called to Jim, but I couldn't hear any answer.

78
00:05:45.737 --> 00:05:47.398
Finally I swam to shore.

79
00:05:48.299 --> 00:05:51.460
Then I started walking and I came to a big log house.

80
00:05:52.421 --> 00:05:57.044
Suddenly a pack of dogs jumped out of the darkness, howling and barking at me.
